PS5 HDMI: RGB range flag encoded correctly (CTA-861 Q1:Q0)

v1.5.0's full-range fix wrote the hdmi.rgb_range value itself into the AVI
InfoFrame's quantization field, but CTA-861 codes it as 0 default, 1 limited,
2 full: "1 = full" (the default) went out as LIMITED. TVs that follow the flag
(VCDB QS=1, most current TVs) on "Auto" then treated the GPU's full-range RGB as
limited: too dark, crushed shadows, at every resolution (Gilles, LG). The
parameter keeps its meaning (1 full, 2 limited, 0 no flag), now mapped to the
right codes.
---

--- a/drivers/ps5/hdmi.c
+++ b/drivers/ps5/hdmi.c
@@ -1147,8 +1147,17 @@
 	}
 	/* Enable IT content */
 	i2c_mask(0x7226, 1 << 7, 0x80);
-	/* RGB quantization range (Q1:Q0), only meaningful for RGB output */
-	i2c_mask(0x7226, avi_rgb && rgb_range <= 2 ? rgb_range << 2 : 0x00, 0x0c);
+	/*
+	 * RGB quantization range (Q1:Q0), only meaningful for RGB output. CTA-861 codes:
+	 * 0 default, 1 limited, 2 full. Earlier versions wrote the parameter value itself, so
+	 * "1 = full" went out as LIMITED: TVs that follow the flag (VCDB QS=1, e.g. LG) on
+	 * "Auto" showed full-range RGB as limited, too dark at every resolution (an LG TV).
+	 */
+	{
+		static const u8 q_code[] = { 0, 2, 1 };	/* rgb_range 0 default, 1 full, 2 limited */
+
+		i2c_mask(0x7226, avi_rgb && rgb_range < ARRAY_SIZE(q_code) ? q_code[rgb_range] << 2 : 0x00, 0x0c);
+	}
 	/* Set content type (game) */
 	i2c_mask(0x7228, 3 << 4, 0x30);
 	i2c_write(0x7204, 0x40);
